{
  "_why": "Which benchmarks a change makes worth re-running. Deliberately coarse: a benchmark run for nothing costs minutes, one not run hides a regression, so the globs are directories and over-include on purpose. 'benchmarks' are BenchmarkDotNet classes, measured against themselves; 'harnesses' are the cross-language pairs, which is where the claim against rapidfuzz is actually measured. tools/check_bench_map.py refuses either kind if this file does not name it. A harness also names the corpus generators it needs: those corpora are written rather than committed, and a runner has none of them. CrossLang/ is not one 'always' unit (#351): Harness.cs is the base every cross-language file builds on, but each comparison's own file -- PersistenceCrossLang.cs, MetricsCrossLang.cs, and so on -- belongs to that one harness's own sources, not to all four. Measured: a single line added to PersistenceCrossLang.cs previously matched 'always' and selected all 15 benchmark classes and all 4 harnesses; it now selects one. 'diagnostics' are the third kind: subcommands that answer a question a lot asks once, so the nightly deliberately never runs them and they carry no sources. They are listed anyway, because a bench_*.py that nothing names is otherwise indistinguishable from a harness somebody forgot to map -- which is the finding this file exists to raise.",
  "always": [
    "src/Shared/**",
    "bench/Lodestar.Text.Benchmarks/BenchCorpus.cs",
    "bench/Lodestar.Text.Benchmarks/Program.cs",
    "bench/Lodestar.Text.Benchmarks/CrossLang/Harness.cs",
    "bench/python/harness.py",
    "bench/compare.py",
    "bench/corpus/**"
  ],
  "diagnostics": [
    { "subcommand": "roc-parallel" },
    { "subcommand": "save-phases" },
    { "subcommand": "heap-warmth", "python": "bench/python/bench_heap_warmth.py" }
  ],
